 Going into an equation as we do in three dimensions can be tricky when studying geometry, so we start by projecting
\[\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\ 3t \\ 1 - t \end{pmatrix}\]onto the plane $x = z,$ which is of the form $x - z = 0.$  Since $x = 1 + t,$ $z = 1 - t,$ and $y = 3t,$ we get
\[(1 + t) - (1 - t) = 2t.\]Since $t = (y/3),$ the equation $2t$ depends only on $y,$ and the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the plane $x = z$ is
\[\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\ 3t \\ 1 - t \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + y/3 \\ y \\ 1 - y/3 \end{pmatrix}.\]This projection is also in the form
\[\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + y/3 \\ y \\ 1 - y/3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + y \begin{pmatrix} 1/3 \\ 1 \\ -1/3 \end{pmatrix}.\]That is, $\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\ 3t \\ 1 - t \end{pmatrix}$ depends only on $y,$ so it must lie in the plane we are interested in.  We project $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 1/3 \\ 1 \\ -1/3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto the plane $x = z.$

